ince it wasn't going to make a  difference in our recommendations we did  not recommend an antigen at that period  of time  i do have to ask you there are so many  places across this country where people  just aren't wearing masks now you're  asking them to wear masks how do we get  this across to people that you know if  you've been exposed or you've tested  positive you still have to wear that  mask for a certain period of time  right well and that's actually a really  important point and that is we really do  need people to follow these  recommendations to for them to work but  i would also say those people who are  not masking who are out and about are  probably not the ones who are isolating  either so what we really wanted to do is  make sure that these recommendations  were more easily followed we shortened  the time to encourage people to do the  right thing especially those who are  infected we don't want them out and  about when they are maximally infectious  dr welinski i want to get back to the  rapid antigen testing for a second if  they were more readily available in this  country would you have included a  testing requirement after that five day  period  so to be clear we have  we did not include a testing requirement  in isolation because we were not clear  it was not clear what we would do with  the information when we had it and  because the fda has not authorized that  antigen test for that use  we do absolutely recommend an antigen  test in our quarantine recommendations  for anybody who after they've been  exposed at five days so this really had  nothing to do with supply it had  everything to do with knowing what we  would do with the information when we  got it dr wilinski thank you so much for  joining us  thanks for having me
 

